</code></pre> <p>Dart can not load files at run time in an AOT binary. So the CLI keeps a list of your migrations in <code>db/migration/migrations.dart</code>. You do not edit it. The server can run the migrations at start. See Migration.</p>

Database <code>Driver</code> Default driver Fallback Dart driver Pool Good for Postgres <code>Driver.postgres</code> <code>dox_postgres_native</code> <code>postgres</code> Yes Production MySQL 8+ and MariaDB <code>Driver.mysql</code> <code>dox_mysql_native</code> <code>mysql_dart</code> Yes Production SQLite <code>Driver.sqlite</code> <code>sqlite3</code> none No, one connection per isolate Development, tests, small apps <p>Native drivers</p> <p>Postgres and MySQL use a native (Rust) driver by default. It is faster when many requests run at the same time. You do not change any config for it.</p> <p>The ORM uses the Dart driver in these cases:</p> <ul> <li>The native library can not load on your system. The ORM prints one warning line.</li> <li>Postgres uses TLS (<code>useSSL: true</code> or <code>extra['sslMode']</code>). The native Postgres driver has no TLS yet.</li> <li>You ask for it: set <code>DOX_DB_DRIVER=dart</code> for all connections,   or <code>extra: {'native': false}</code> for one connection.</li> </ul> <p>See the Postgres and MySQL pages.</p> <p>Dox picks the driver of a connection like this:</p> <pre><code>flowchart TD
    A["Driver.postgres or Driver.mysql"] --&gt; B{"DOX_DB_DRIVER=dart&lt;br&gt;or extra native: false?"}
    B --&gt;|yes| X["Dart driver"]
    B --&gt;|no| C{"Postgres with TLS?&lt;br&gt;useSSL or sslMode"}
    C --&gt;|yes| X
    C --&gt;|no| L{"Native library loads?"}
    L --&gt;|no| W["Dart driver&lt;br&gt;prints one warning"]
    L --&gt;|yes| N["Native Rust driver"]
    Q["Driver.sqlite"] --&gt; S["sqlite3&lt;br&gt;one connection per isolate"]</code></pre>

lib/app/models/blog/blog.model.dart<pre><code>import 'package:dox_orm/dox_orm.dart';

part 'blog.model.g.dart'; // (1)!

@DoxModel() // (2)!
class Blog extends BlogGenerator { // (3)!
  @Column() // (4)!
  String? title;

  @Column(beforeSave: makeSlug) // (5)!
  String? slug;

  @Column()
  String? status;

  @Column(name: 'body') // (6)!
  String? description;

  @Column(name: 'user_id')
  int? userId;

  static String? makeSlug(Map&lt;String, dynamic&gt; map) {
    return map['title']?.toString().toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-');
  }
}
</code></pre> <ol> <li><code>build_runner</code> writes this file. It holds the generated <code>BlogGenerator</code> class.</li> <li>Marks the class as a model. The options, like <code>table</code> and <code>softDelete</code>, go here.     The table is <code>blog</code> by default: the class name in snake_case.</li> <li>The generated class gives the model <code>id</code>, <code>createdAt</code> and <code>updatedAt</code>, and the query builder methods.</li> <li>One <code>@Column()</code> field is one table column. The column name is the field name in snake_case.</li> <li><code>beforeSave</code> runs this static function before a save. It gets all values and returns the value for this column.</li> <li> <p><code>name</code> sets the column name when it is not the snake_case of the field. <code>description</code> reads and writes <code>body</code>.</p> </li> <li> <p>The model gets an <code>id</code> field, and <code>createdAt</code> and <code>updatedAt</code> fields. You do not declare them.</p> </li> <li>Each <code>@Column()</code> field is one table column.</li> <li>Make every column field nullable (<code>String?</code>, <code>int?</code>). A new model has no values yet.</li> </ol>

lib/app/http/serializers/blog.serializer.dart<pre><code>import 'package:dox_core/dox_core.dart';

import '../../../app/models/blog/blog.model.dart';

class BlogSerializer extends Serializer&lt;Blog&gt; {
  BlogSerializer(super.data);

  @override
  Map&lt;String, dynamic&gt; convert(Blog m) {
    return &lt;String, dynamic&gt;{
      'uid': m.id,
      'title_en': m.title,
    };
  }
}
</code></pre> lib/app/http/controllers/blog.controller.dart<pre><code>class BlogController {
  Future&lt;BlogSerializer&gt; index(DoxRequest req) async {
    List&lt;Blog&gt; blogs = await Blog().all();
    return BlogSerializer(blogs); // a list
  }

  Future&lt;BlogSerializer&gt; show(DoxRequest req, String id) async {
    Blog? blog = await Blog().find(id);
    return BlogSerializer(blog); // one model
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>The response of <code>show</code>:</p> <pre><code>{
  "uid": 1,
  "title_en": "This is title"
}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>convert</code> runs once for each model.</li> <li>Give a list, and the response is a JSON list.   Give one model, and the response is one JSON object.</li> <li>Give <code>null</code> (for example, <code>find</code> found nothing), and the response body is <code>null</code>.</li> <li>The type in <code>Serializer&lt;Blog&gt;</code> must be the model type. Other values are ignored.</li> </ul>

